          "question": "I get migraines maybe six times a month.",
          "answer": "Based on your description of migraines occurring six times a month, the system assesses this as a **routine** concern. There is no emergency pattern that matches this description. However, this does not mean the symptom is not serious. If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should still seek medical advice.\n\nMigraine is a recurrent neurological disorder, often involving headache, sensory sensitivity, and nausea. Chronic migraine is defined as experiencing headaches on fifteen or more days per month, with at least eight of those having migraine features, for more than three months, as noted in the \"Migraine\" document. Your described frequency of six times a month falls below this definition of chronic migraine.\n\nHowever, it is important to consider that frequent use of acute migraine medication can sometimes lead to a \"medication-overuse headache,\" which can convert an episodic pattern into a chronic one. Guidelines suggest that using certain acute medications (like triptans, ergots, opioids, and combination analgesics) fewer than ten days per month helps prevent this (from the \"Migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits\" guideline). Your frequency of six migraines a month is within a range where discussing medication use with a clinician would be helpful to ensure you are not at risk for medication-overuse headache.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
