          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is that enough to need something preventive?",
          "answer": "Preventive treatment for migraine is generally offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not sufficient. The guidance suggests considering prevention when there are four or more migraine days per month with some disability. It can also be considered with fewer attacks if the disability is severe, if acute treatment is ineffective or contraindicated, or if the individual prefers it. The goal of preventive treatment is to reduce the frequency and severity of migraines, with a 50% reduction in migraine days being a common definition of a successful treatment response.\n\nGeneral health and lifestyle factors that are often addressed in migraine management include maintaining regular sleep patterns, ensuring meal regularity, and avoiding overuse of acute migraine medication. These factors contribute to overall health and can influence migraine frequency and severity.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: guideline-migraine-acute-and-preventive-treatment, condition-migraine, lifestyle-migraine-sleep]",
